(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-hf-diuretics-acei-beta-blockers-quiz"]={"title":"Heart Failure: Diuretics, ACE Inhibitors & Beta Blockers Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which two neurohormonal systems are activated as cardiac output falls in heart failure?","choices":["Parasympathetic and thyroid systems","Insulin and glucagon","Sympathetic and renin-angiotensin-aldosterone systems","Thyroid and growth hormone axes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Falling cardiac output activates both systems, causing vasoconstriction, faster heart rate and sodium and water retention. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A patient with heart failure measures a blood pressure of 210 over 120 and is breathless. Which precipitant fits best?","choices":["Uncontrolled hypertension","Cardiac arrhythmia","Pulmonary infection","Lack of compliance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Uncontrolled hypertension raises afterload and is a recognized precipitant of decompensation. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A patient asks whether his loop diuretic will help him live longer. What is the best answer?","choices":["It slows heart failure progression","It must be taken daily to prevent death","It reverses the ventricular damage","It eases symptoms but not survival"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Diuretics give symptomatic relief only, with no evidence of reduced progression or mortality. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Why do thicker, stiffer ventricles lower cardiac output?","choices":["They contract with too much force","They relax poorly, so filling falls","They empty the blood too quickly","They lose their ability to hold blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stiff ventricles relax less efficiently, so ventricular filling and cardiac output decrease. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A patient with class III heart failure takes a loop diuretic and an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor. Which drug should be added to lower mortality?","choices":["Digoxin","Carvedilol","Hydrochlorothiazide","Atenolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carvedilol is one of the three beta blockers with a mortality benefit and is first-line therapy. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which life-threatening reaction involves swelling of the lips, tongue and throat during angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor therapy?","choices":["Peripheral edema","Pulmonary edema","Ascites","Angioedema"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Angioedema is a recognized and potentially dangerous adverse reaction to these drugs. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What is the most common cause of heart failure?","choices":["Uncontrolled hypertension","Idiopathic dilated cardiomyopathy","Alcoholic cardiomyopathy","Ischemic heart disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ischemic heart disease, including myocardial infarction, accounts for the largest share of heart failure cases. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A patient with stable heart failure develops a new rapid, irregular pulse and worsening breathlessness. Which precipitant fits best?","choices":["Lack of compliance","Cardiac arrhythmia","Uncontrolled hypertension","Emotional stress"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An arrhythmia, such as a new rapid rhythm, is a recognized precipitant of decompensation. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which respiratory adverse effect is associated with angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors?","choices":["Bronchospasm","Hemoptysis","Cough","Pleural effusion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cough is a recognized adverse effect of these drugs. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which blood pressure problem can angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors cause in heart failure?","choices":["Hypotension","Hypertensive crisis","Orthostatic hypertension","Pulmonary hypertension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypotension is a recognized problem, particularly with other blood pressure-lowering drugs. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which drug class is first-line therapy for heart failure of functional class II to IV, alongside angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors?","choices":["Digoxin","Beta blockers","Thiazide diuretics","Calcium channel blockers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers are first-line therapy for heart failure of functional class II to IV. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which dietary advice is part of non-drug therapy for heart failure?","choices":["Restrict sodium and fluid","Increase sodium to maintain volume","Drink extra water to flush the kidneys","Eat no protein"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Limiting dietary sodium and fluid reduces edema and helps functional status. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"In which state should a heart failure patient be before a beta blocker is started?","choices":["Clinically stable","Acutely decompensated","Hypotensive and congested","Receiving intravenous inotropes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The patient should be stable before a beta blocker is initiated. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which compensatory response in heart failure increases preload?","choices":["Vasoconstriction","Sodium and water retention","Left ventricular hypertrophy","Bradycardia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Retaining sodium and water expands blood volume and raises preload. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A patient on a loop diuretic is started on an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor. What should be monitored?","choices":["Serum calcium and phosphate","Blood glucose and ketones","Serum potassium and renal function","White blood cell count"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The inhibitor can raise potassium and impair renal function, and the loop pushes potassium the other way. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A man with heart failure ran out of his medications two weeks ago and now has marked breathlessness and swelling. Which precipitant of decompensation fits best?","choices":["Cardiac arrhythmia","Pulmonary infection","Acute anginal chest pain","Lack of compliance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Not taking prescribed therapy is a common precipitant of decompensation. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which drug class both slows heart failure progression and decreases mortality?","choices":["Loop diuretics","Thiazide diuretics","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors","Cardiac glycosides such as digoxin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In heart failure with reduced ejection fraction these drugs slow progression and decrease mortality, which is why they are mandatory there. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which two drug classes should a patient with heart failure and a reduced ejection fraction be on irrespective of symptoms?","choices":["Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor and beta blocker","Loop diuretic and cardiac glycoside","Thiazide diuretic and dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","Loop diuretic and thiazide diuretic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients should be on both classes irrespective of symptoms, since both lower mortality. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Why were beta blockers classically considered contraindicated in heart failure?","choices":["They raise serum potassium","They cause fluid loss","They can worsen heart failure","They block the renin system"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lowering heart rate and contractility can decompensate a failing heart, so they were once avoided. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which hemodynamic improvement do beta blockers eventually produce in heart failure?","choices":["A rise in ejection fraction","A fall in ejection fraction","A rise in resting heart rate","A rise in cardiac preload"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With long-term therapy beta blockers improve hemodynamics, including an increase in ejection fraction. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Why are thiazide diuretics unsuitable for most patients with heart failure?","choices":["They raise potassium dangerously","They are not potent enough","They increase preload","They worsen contractility"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Thiazides are not potent enough to clear the fluid of most heart failure patients. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which exposure is a recognized cause of cardiomyopathy leading to heart failure?","choices":["Moderate exercise","Low sodium intake","Vitamin C intake","Alcohol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Alcoholic, viral and hypertrophic cardiomyopathies, and drug-induced damage, are recognized causes of heart failure. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 45"}]};
